agents/ — generic executor subagents
=======================================
(File is extensionless on purpose: the agent loader scans *.md, and a
README.md without agent frontmatter risks a startup-breaking parse error.)

What lives here
---------------
One file per model, plus one file per role-specialized tier. The bulk of the
roster is GENERIC executors: the model is pinned, the role comes entirely
from the task prompt. Two single-model tiers carry their role in the file
instead — `verifier-*` (runs tests/linters/harnesses, reports results) and
`researcher-*` (fetches external sources, reports with URLs and quotes) —
because their permission shapes differ. Tiers stay single-model: execution
output is deterministic and research fan-out is serial, so neither benefits
from model diversity.

Naming: <model-slug>-<tier> for executors; <role>-<model-slug>-<tier> for the
specialized tiers. Cost tiers are COST, not capability:
  -free   no-cost models (7 executors; verifier and researcher also free)
  -lite   budget paid, currently deepseek-v4-pro only
  -paid   flagship paid (glm, kimi, qwen)
Dots in model ids become hyphens; a redundant trailing -free is collapsed
(e.g. mimo-v2-5-free, not mimo-v2-5-free-free).

Prompt: role frame + follow-the-task-prompt + one pointer line to
protocols/executor-protocol.md (shared behavior: BLOCKED, denial handling,
sandbox discipline). Deliberately minimal — output formats and specs belong
in per-task briefs, which is where the review-orchestration and adviser-chat
skills put them.

Permissions (three profiles — least privilege per role)
-------------------------------------------------------
Executors observe and report; only the primary acts. Base profile (all
files): read tools, instructions/ + protocols/ + /tmp/ dirs, git read-only
subcommands (plain and `git -C` forms), read-only text tools
(wc/sort/uniq/cut/tr/jq/rg/grep, space-required form so bare invocations
can't hang on stdin). Denied everywhere: edit (covers writes), task
(executors are terminal), all other bash.
- Executors: base profile, webfetch/websearch denied. Hermetic by
  construction — output derives only from repo and prompt.
- Verifier: base profile plus execution-shaped bash (pytest, npm test,
  go/cargo test, tsc, ruff, eslint, node --check, node *.js*). No direct
  edit tools; execution can still write (test logs), so the promise is
  containment, not incorruptibility. Project-specific runners belong in
  per-project agent files, not here.
- Researcher: base profile plus webfetch/websearch. Treats fetched content
  as untrusted data; every external claim needs URL + verbatim quote.
Rationale: `sed`/`awk`/`find`/`xargs`/interpreters stay denied on executors:
their safe and destructive modes are indistinguishable by prefix. Verified
live: chained commands (`git status; echo X`, pipes) are denied despite
prefix match — the allowlist is enforced, not theater. Each tier's boundary
is restated subagent-side in ../protocols/ (executor-, verifier-,
researcher-protocol.md). Rules 1–4 are identical across the three files —
keep them in sync when editing; the sync note lives here, not in the
protocol files, because those are read on every spawn.

Adding a model
--------------
1. Copy the nearest-tier file. Set `model:` to the exact provider/model id and
   rewrite the one-line description ([TIER] + blurb + generic-executor line).
2. Keep the permission block and two-sentence prompt byte-identical.
3. Add the new `subagent_type` to the Tiers list in
   ../skill/review-orchestration/SKILL.md (full names only — short names do
   not resolve).
4. Restart opencode (agents/skills/permissions load at startup).

Retiring a model: delete its file, remove it from the skill tier list,
restart. If in doubt, keep the file — a stale roster entry costs nothing
until invoked, while a missing one breaks saved prompts that name it.
